# Watchdog health and stuck runs

When the user asks about runs, jobs, queue status, restarts, or whether ensemble members are progressing, check the automated watchdog too — not just `qstat`.

From `drought-ensemble/`:

```bash
python3 ensemble_running/watchdog/restart_watchdog.py --heartbeat
python3 ensemble_running/watchdog/restart_watchdog.py --list-stuck
```

## Heartbeat

`--heartbeat` checks both halves of the pipeline separately:

- **cron decides** (`logs/last_status.txt`, written on the cron host each tick — submit or skip)
- **completed scans** (`logs/last_cycle.txt`, written by the Casper job)

Cron may **skip** Casper when `logs/wake_state.txt` is clean / still watching queued jobs, so scan age can grow up to `max_idle_scan_hours` (24 h). Cron status must still refresh every few hours. Either half going stale beyond its limit means runs are silently not being restarted. This happened for six days in Aug 2026: cron fired on schedule but every `qsub` failed with `command not found`, so no scan ever ran and nothing surfaced it. **If the heartbeat is unhealthy, tell the user immediately and prominently** — a healthy-looking `qstat` does not mean the watchdog is alive.

## Stuck sequences

If `--list-stuck` reports `stuck_same_year` targets, those failed twice on the same year tip and will **not** be auto-resubmitted until the sequence tombstone under `domains/<domain>/raw_runs/.watchdog_tombstones/` is cleared. Report them, and treat a repeated same-year failure as a likely real bug rather than a transient walltime kill.
